Sval: a crash keeps no minidump while private browsing is in use

A minidump holds the crashed process's stacks and the memory they point at,
so one written while a private window is open can hold fragments of its
pages, and it stays on disk after the window closes. Sval keeps nothing of a
private window, so such a crash keeps no minidump, and with no minidump
there is no .extra file, no crash event and no crash reporter window.

One flag says so, in toolkit/crashreporter/SvalCrashPrivacy.cpp. A content
process sets it when its remote type is a private one, and its exception
filter then ends the process in place of asking for a minidump. The parent
process sets it while a private browsing context exists or
browser.privatebrowsing.autostart is on. Its own filter then ends the
process too, it takes no paired minidump of a hung child and itself, and it
removes the minidump the crash helper wrote for any child that serves every
window: the GPU, RDD, socket, utility, VR and GMP processes, and a content
process that is not one site's, such as the one for local files or for
extensions. A content process for a normal window's sites holds no private
page, and its crash keeps its report.

On macOS and Linux the filters call _exit. A filter that returns false
hands the crash to the system's handler, and a sandboxed content process on
macOS then stays suspended and never exits. On Windows the filters return
AbortWithoutMinidump, which ends the process the same way.

TakeMinidumpForChild takes the crashed process's type to tell the two
apart.

